::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A report starts with a transportation problem, gives evidence from rider complaints, and ends with a proposed pilot schedule. What structure is most evident?
{
=A. Problem-solution.#Correct
~B. Character conflict.#Incorrect
~C. Random listing.#Incorrect
~D. Historical biography.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A claim says evening library hours support working students. Evidence includes work schedules, study room use, and requests for later access. What is the best evaluation?
{
~A. The support is unrelated.#Incorrect
=B. The support is relevant and develops the central idea.#Correct
~C. The support is weak because it is not fictional.#Incorrect
~D. The support proves every student needs evening hours.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A public notice uses direct commands and simple sentences during a storm warning. What purpose does the style serve?
{
~A. To confuse readers.#Incorrect
~B. To entertain readers.#Incorrect
=C. To make urgent instructions clear for a broad audience.#Correct
~D. To hide the author's message.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A uses cost data for a proposal. Text B uses a personal story for the same proposal. Which statement is best?
{
~A. Both texts use identical evidence.#Incorrect
=D. Text A may fit decision-makers, while Text B may build public concern.#Correct
~C. Text B is invalid because stories cannot persuade.#Incorrect
~B. Text A has no rhetoric.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Passage: "The proposal is not an expense; it is an investment in access." What rhetorical choice is used?
{
~B. Chronological ordering.#Incorrect
=A. Reframing through contrast.#Correct
~C. Technical definition.#Incorrect
~D. Neutral description only.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A study says the program increased workshop attendance but did not prove higher grades. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. The program caused students to earn higher grades.#Incorrect
~C. The program had no effect on attendance.#Incorrect
=B. The program was connected to higher workshop attendance, not proven grade gains.#Correct
~D. The study was only about sports.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A writer calls a change a "limited pilot." What does this phrase suggest?
{
=C. A cautious temporary test.#Correct
~B. A permanent guarantee.#Incorrect
~A. A hostile accusation.#Incorrect
~D. A fictional event.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is clearest and most formal?
{
~A. The policy, it helps people and stuff.#Incorrect
=D. The policy supports residents by expanding access to needed services.#Correct
~C. Helping everyone with things is what it does.#Incorrect
~B. The thing is good because yeah.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which presentation statement best evaluates an argument?
{
~B. I thought the passage was about buses.#Incorrect
=A. The argument is effective because its evidence and cautious tone fit a policy audience.#Correct
~C. I liked the topic.#Incorrect
~D. There were words in the passage.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_Pre_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Passage: Text A uses data about missed appointments. Text B uses a patient's story. What is the best comparison?
{
=B. Text A measures the problem, while Text B humanizes its effect.#Correct
~A. Text B has no purpose.#Incorrect
~C. Text A is emotional because it uses numbers.#Incorrect
~D. Both texts are unrelated.#Incorrect
}
